# Git Commands

Rules for using git commands safely and effectively.

<rule>
name: git_commands
description: Standards for using git commands safely and effectively
filters:
  - type: content
    pattern: "git.*diff|git.*log|git.*status|git.*blame|git.*show"

actions:
  - type: reject
    conditions:
      - pattern: "git (diff|log|status|blame|show)(?! .*--no-pager)"
        message: "Always use --no-pager with git commands that might produce interactive output"
      - pattern: "git (?!--no-pager).*\\| less"
        message: "Do not pipe git output to less/more - use --no-pager instead"

  - type: suggest
    message: |
      When using git commands that might produce interactive output:

      1. ALWAYS use --no-pager for commands that might page output:
         ```bash
         # Bad
         git diff
         git log
         git status -vv
         git blame file.txt
         git show HEAD

         # Good
         git --no-pager diff
         git --no-pager log
         git --no-pager status -vv
         git --no-pager blame file.txt
         git --no-pager show HEAD
         ```

      2. Place --no-pager BEFORE the git subcommand:
         ```bash
         # Bad
         git diff --no-pager  # Won't work

         # Good
         git --no-pager diff  # Correct placement
         ```

      3. Common commands that need --no-pager:
         - git diff
         - git log
         - git blame
         - git show
         - git status (with -v or -vv)
         - git help

      4. When writing scripts or automation:
         - Always use --no-pager to ensure non-interactive output
         - Consider using specific git plumbing commands
         - Use -n/--max-count to limit output when appropriate
         - Avoid piping to pagers (less, more, etc.)

      5. For large outputs:
         - Use specific paths/files instead of entire repo
         - Add commit range limits
         - Use --max-count or similar options
         - Consider using more specific commands

examples:
  - input: |
      # Bad: Will invoke pager
      git diff HEAD~1
      git log -p
      git status -vv
      git show HEAD
      git diff | less

      # Good: Non-interactive output
      git --no-pager diff HEAD~1
      git --no-pager log -p
      git --no-pager status -vv
      git --no-pager show HEAD
      git --no-pager diff  # Direct output, no pager needed
    output: "Git commands that won't invoke pager"

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 1.1
</rule>

